### Final scope

- The unchanged dependency-free `local + dry-run` path remains authoritative.
- Exactly three combinations are supported:
  - `local + dry-run`
  - `local + test-inbox`
  - `asynq + dry-run`
- `asynq + test-inbox` is explicitly refused before campaign work.

### Requirements clarified

- Test-inbox runs:
  - accept only deterministic synthetic fixtures of at most 10 records;
  - validate confirmation and destination allowlisting before any work;
  - reject supplied recipient files;
  - report guard failures as `failure` with zero campaign work and delivery attempts;
  - require confirmed transport acceptance for completion;
  - classify ambiguous acceptance separately without automatic retries.
- Asynq runs:
  - accept only solution-generated synthetic fixtures;
  - require Redis and at least one separately started worker;
  - never silently start an in-process worker;
  - use application-owned idempotency under at-least-once execution;
  - define retry exhaustion, graceful shutdown, cancellation closure, late-task behavior, and completion deadlines;
  - preserve only trustworthy accounting when Redis state becomes unknown;
  - report known-enqueued, known-terminal, and unknown-task totals separately when full reconciliation is impossible;
  - start with one queue unless measured workload classes justify weighted queues.
- Restored the pre-existing deferred questions for input format, cancellation bounds, masking, and memory measurement.
- Updated F5 and AE18 to explicitly cover generated fixtures and separate worker operation.

